WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:13.471
Hi there, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:14.311 --> 00:00:19.693
Have you ever heard of penguins living near the equator or seen birds with bright blue feet?

3
00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:24.794
Did you know that some animals can live to be 150 years old?

4
00:00:25.854 --> 00:00:32.300
Well, in this week's landmarks lesson, we learned about the Galapagos Islands, which are famous for all the

5
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.581
really unusual animals that live there!

6
00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:42.063
The Galapagos Islands are located on the equator, about one thousand kilometers west of mainland South America.

7
00:00:43.003 --> 00:00:50.966
This archipelago, or group of islands, consists of 13 big islands, 6 smaller ones, and a whole bunch

8
00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:51.726
of tiny ones.

9
00:00:53.546 --> 00:00:59.808
Scientists believe that the islands were formed millions of years ago by eruptions from underwater volcanoes.

10
00:01:00.908 --> 00:01:05.510
The islands' shores are barren and rocky, but there's plenty of vegetation inland.

11
00:01:06.611 --> 00:01:09.852
Some areas are covered with huge cacti that look like trees.

12
00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:17.936
There are also dense forests and humid swamps that create enough fog to completely hide the islands sometimes.

13
00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:24.799
Mr. Fay said that's probably why the Galapagos Islands were a popular hideout for British pirates in the

14
00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:25.800
seventeenth century.

15
00:01:26.856 --> 00:01:33.240
Nobody actually lived on the islands, so after attacking Spanish ships or settlements, the pirates would retreat to

16
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.284
them. With all that thick fog, the pirates couldn't be spotted by people aboard passing ships!

17
00:01:41.165 --> 00:01:44.547
Alex and Jake thought the stories about the pirates were really cool.

18
00:01:45.608 --> 00:01:50.952
But if you ask me, the most interesting thing about the Galapagos Islands is the wildlife that lives

19
00:01:51.012 --> 00:01:57.246
there. Many of the animal species are endemic, which means you won't find them anywhere else in the

20
00:01:57.286 --> 00:02:02.848
world. Not only that, most of these species vary from one island to another.

21
00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:10.871
When the naturalist Charles Darwin visited the Galapagos Islands in 1835, he was amazed by how unique and

22
00:02:10.911 --> 00:02:13.012
diverse the animals on the islands are.

23
00:02:14.013 --> 00:02:20.775
Darwin noticed, for example, that each island has its own distinct species of finch, with its own unique

24
00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:21.976
beak shape and size.

25
00:02:23.916 --> 00:02:28.598
One finch species is even smarter than the others—it uses tools!

26
00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:35.921
But it doesn't use power saws or hammers or anything; this finch uses sticks to dig for bugs.

27
00:02:36.761 --> 00:02:37.942
Pretty incredible, huh?

28
00:02:39.042 --> 00:02:46.826
Anyway, Darwin's observations about the finches and other animals on the Galapagos Islands eventually inspired his famous theories

29
00:02:46.886 --> 00:02:48.246
about natural selection.

30
00:02:49.227 --> 00:02:52.328
Many other kinds of birds live on the Galapagos Islands too.

31
00:02:53.408 --> 00:02:59.451
The blue-footed booby is my favorite because it has bright blue feet and looks very fashionable!

32
00:03:00.531 --> 00:03:05.813
Mr. Fay also told us about a strange bird called the magnificent frigate bird.

33
00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:09.275
The male has a huge red pouch under its beak.

34
00:03:10.355 --> 00:03:16.578
And get this: the magnificent frigate bird can't take off from flat ground—it has to run down a

35
00:03:16.638 --> 00:03:18.359
hill to build up enough speed!

36
00:03:19.479 --> 00:03:20.480
How weird is that?

37
00:03:22.188 --> 00:03:27.692
There's also one type of bird you'd never expect to see living so close to the equator—the Galapagos

38
00:03:27.812 --> 00:03:31.835
penguin. But it's not easy for them to survive there.

39
00:03:32.855 --> 00:03:38.119
These penguins are threatened whenever the temperature of the water rises by even a few degrees.

40
00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.664
The most famous animal on the Galapagos Islands is probably the Galapagos giant tortoise.

41
00:03:45.864 --> 00:03:52.108
In fact, the islands are named after these tortoises; the Spanish word for "tortoise" is galapago.

42
00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:59.912
These big reptiles can grow to be over 1.5 meters long and weigh up to 250 kilograms.

43
00:04:00.912 --> 00:04:04.794
They can also go a whole year without eating or drinking anything.

44
00:04:05.854 --> 00:04:10.736
But that's nothing compared to how long they can live—150 years!

45
00:04:12.577 --> 00:04:16.539
Another fascinating reptile on the islands is the marine iguana.

46
00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:19.880
Usually iguanas stay on land.

47
00:04:20.888 --> 00:04:26.913
But scientists believe that when food became scarce, some Galapagos iguanas started hunting in the water.

48
00:04:27.994 --> 00:04:32.558
Over many generations these iguanas evolved into great swimmers.

49
00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:39.985
The marine iguanas can swim about fifteen meters deep and stay underwater for almost a half an hour.

50
00:04:41.106 --> 00:04:46.110
It's the only lizard in the world that swims in the ocean, and it can only be found

51
00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:47.712
on the Galapagos Islands.

52
00:04:48.932 --> 00:04:54.896
As you've probably figured out by now, the Galapagos Islands are a pretty important and unique landmark that's

53
00:04:54.956 --> 00:04:55.937
worth preserving.

54
00:04:57.038 --> 00:05:01.100
Luckily the islands were turned into a national park in 1959.

55
00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:05.644
Even the waters surrounding the islands are protected as a reserve.

56
00:05:07.365 --> 00:05:10.927
A big threat to the Galapagos Islands today is tourism.

57
00:05:11.808 --> 00:05:17.612
Over 100,000 people visit the archipelago each year, threatening its fragile ecosystems.

58
00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.744
Scientists at the Charles Darwin Research Station, which was also established in 1959, study the islands' plant and

59
00:05:25.844 --> 00:05:30.867
animal life and work closely with the Galapagos National Park Service to protect the area.

60
00:05:31.708 --> 00:05:37.791
Tourists have to follow trails, and park workers carefully monitor the number of people allowed on the islands.

61
00:05:38.772 --> 00:05:44.956
Park workers also make sure that tour guides are properly licensed so that rules and regulations are strictly

62
00:05:45.016 --> 00:05:50.589
followed. Tourists can't touch the animals or remove anything from the islands.

63
00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:56.411
If you go, though, you'll probably be able to get very close to some of the animals.

64
00:05:57.591 --> 00:06:03.853
Since the islands were uninhabited by people until recently (except for those pirates, of course!), many of the

65
00:06:03.993 --> 00:06:07.834
animals are not afraid of humans and will let you walk right up to them.

66
00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:13.716
Mr. Fay showed us a picture of a person sitting in front of a sea lion on the

67
00:06:13.756 --> 00:06:18.054
beach! There are also plenty of animals to see in the water.

68
00:06:18.894 --> 00:06:24.957
Divers around the islands can swim alongside fish, sharks, morays, and even whales.

69
00:06:26.238 --> 00:06:31.581
I don't know about you, but I think it would be totally amazing to swim alongside a huge

70
00:06:31.661 --> 00:06:32.441
whale shark.

71
00:06:34.262 --> 00:06:36.303
After the landmarks lesson, Mr.

72
00:06:36.363 --> 00:06:41.826
Fay gave us a fun assignment: we have to write a report on our favorite Galapagos animal.

73
00:06:42.927 --> 00:06:44.988
I picked the blue-footed booby, of course.

74
00:06:45.864 --> 00:06:49.647
So I started doing a little research, and guess what I found out.

75
00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:52.769
There's also a red-footed booby!

76
00:06:52.789 --> 00:06:56.512
Hmm blue feet or red feet.

77
00:06:57.473 --> 00:06:59.674
Now I can't decide which one I like better!
